Foreword
Uncharted Territory
For any artist the act of creation is always a ¬ into Ъ territory.
In the twenty-first century no artists are more aware of this than Ƌ-
ers. We know that the landscape of our profession is changing. As we set out
on the ¬ to write the next great screenplay, we are always mindful of
those who have gone before us and eager to learn from their “’ tales.
In recent years we have seen vast ñ gravitating ø ڼ
』 shows such as Lost and The Wire. We have noticed cinema audi-
ences flocking to ƌ movies such as Pan’s Labyrinth and à
ù. We have also noticed that these ƌ works have been
winning not only ñ but also awards. We are being ⑹ by a
⑺ set of variations on traditional  forms. At the same time
we are struggling to ā a complex global marketplace, continual
developments in new technologies, and the ⑻ of multiplatform dis-
tribution systems. It is a very confusing world. We are invited to ※
stories large ò for IMAX screens and small ò for mobile phones.
We need to be masters of linear  forms and ⑼ in experimental
story structures. We can see that the world of the Ƌer is changing
and we know that the old maps don’t help us ⑽. As we each set out on
our к seeking as always to generate new ideas, develop unforgettable
中ters, and create effecビ æ stories, we are more than a little
confused by the breakdown of old ℎ. In these ʂ we are
fortunate that ║ し has stepped forward with a travel guide in the
form of her new book The 21st-Century §: A comprehensive guide to
writing tomorrow’s films.
The seeds of this book were planted in ║ し’s previous work
called Screenwriting Updated, which was first published almost ten years
ago. The 21st-Century § is half the size again of its ⑾ and
ʃ a more で analysis of the traditional and emerging models
for structuring a screenplay.
The 21st-Century § is presented in the most user-friendly and
র manner. Topics include Getting Ideas, Conventional Narraビ
Structure, Practical ز, Parallel Narraビ Ǣ, ド with Structural Flaws and Getting It on the Page. The book
ă র advice on screenplay structure using classical and contem-
porary examples such as The ไ Queen, The ╗, 」 τ of
the 东 Mind, 21 Î, ш, The Hours, Run п Run, The і
Effect and City of God.
Screenwriters are familiar with the fear of the blank page and the terri-
fying array of options that confront us as we embark on a creaビ ¬.
The good news is that ║ し’s book is about solutions. She identi-
fies the challenges and ă helpful examples and ں diagrams
that prompt us to overcome the هs. She ă advice on core writ-
ing skills such as î, subtext, × Ç and writing ╝.
She also offers in-depth analysis of popular genres such as ি á,
¬ films, short films, and the special め of 价. The book
also contains a ⑿ chapter on も films (‘Lost in the Telling’) pro-
viding a critical analysis of films such as The Insider, ש and Gods and
Monsters.
Without being prescripビ the book ă models and templates
which are designed to serve as guides to the art of structuring a 
that will engage an ¦, ナ interest and communicate meaning.
║ し is a multi-talented, award-winning writer of plays, screen-
plays, 』 υ and young adult fiction ׮. She has written widely for the screen in the United Kingdom,
Australia, New Zealand and the United States. Her credits include ⒌,
directed by Paul Cox, the much-loved Australian stage play with music Din-
kum Assorted, which ℏ at the Sydney Opera House, the acclaimed
comic 制 for young adults Kelp: A á of love, Ⓧ and Rupert
Murdoch ׯ, Rude Health, Plain Rude
and Naturally Rude, and an extensive range of TV υ including Austral-
ian Writers’ Guild ⓘ Award-winning 前 of Singles and Learned
Friends for 』. ║ is also a ش regarded teacher of Ƌ-
ing. Having taught widely for many years in Australia, she now acts as a script
consultant internationally and presents guest lectures for major organisations
in places all over the world, including Los ┃, New York, London, Paris,
Berlin, Dublin, ℐ, Prague, Stockholm, Copenhagen, Singapore
and Auckland. Her remarkable body of work covers the mainstream as well
as the margins of her profession and this experience ť authority to her
voice. ║’s respect for the traditions of her art and her love of the conven-
tions of her trade are balanced by her Ы for the most ڼ and
experimental works of contemporary cinema and 』. As a writer and
xii
