͗ and outlines
The 们 meaning of ╝ and outlines varies a little between film
cultures. Basically, they are 倍 summaries of a proposed film or other
piece of screen υ. They are often selling documents, in which case their
purpose is to provide potential investors of all р with a text that will let
their mind’s eye see the proposed film or ӓ as if they were 交 in
a cinema watching it ³ on screen. a ป for a full-length feature
film or ӓ is normally about 35 pages long, although when used in
Ȗ as a pitch it may be conべ shorter. outlines are shorter and
more succinct. of course, shorter films require shorter ╝. Treat-
ments and outlines are always written in the present tense and their closest
affinity, ۸ally and in function, is with stage ュ.
͗ and outlines are notoriously Ђ to write because at the
same time as being meticulously 们 and Ҟ, unless they are
created just for the writer’s own use or for in-house distribution only, they
must be ‘a good read’—a piece of 倍 that ã off the page. ͗
and outlines must Д Ð, ת and without any ৐, not only
what the camera is seeing but also the order in which it sees it, so that no
mental ‘replays’ have to be done. In addition, the writer must be █
invisible because nothing, including an awareness of the ú’s sensibil-
ity, however unique or acute, must distract よ from the film or ӓ
screening in their heads. as we’ve seen, Ȯ mean the reader cannot
ナ a proper sense of the planned film’s pace or duration. With every
distraction, the film running in the reader’s head risks losing its impact, sus-
pense and ¹ build.
